{:edb/db-before-t 6135 :edb/db-after-t 6136 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539448 50 #inst "2026-10-24T01:55:53.156Z" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186633592 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=68$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=13$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=32,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186633592 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=68$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=13$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=32,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186633592 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-32=-19\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.4}^{2}}{68}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{82}}≈2.035\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -19\\pm 2.035\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186633592 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=13-32=-19\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5.4}^{2}}{68}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{82}}≈2.035\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -19\\pm 2.035\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186633603 1071 "The weights of tomato varieties X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=15\\,\\text{g}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=12\\,\\text{g},$ respectively. A farmer wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights of variety X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights of variety Y. They decide to construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $20$ tomatoes of variety X and $25$ tomatoes of variety Y and found that their mean weights are $99\\,\\text{g}$ and $104\\,\\text{g},$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-5\\pm 8.084.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | -