\,2.408\\,$ | $\\,2.685\\,$ |\n| $48$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.011\\,$ | $\\,2.407\\,$ | $\\,2.682\\,$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.010\\,$ | $\\,2.405\\,$ | $\\,2.680\\,$ |" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186634560 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=49\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=32.5\\,\\text{mpg},\\,n=49,\\,s=4.2\\,\\text{mpg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{48,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{48,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=49-1=48$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/23/2348635fd9697e8610533dbbfc019ba5da5b817381ce1c71cbfe6d74476deea7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{48,0.005}=2.682:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $47$ | $\\,1.300\\,$ | $\\,1.678\\,$ | $\\,2.012\\,$ | $\\,2.408\\,$ | $\\,2.685\\,$ |\n| $48$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.011\\,$ | $\\,2.407\\,$ | $2.682$ |\n| $49$ | $\\,1.299\\,$ | $\\,1.677\\,$ | $\\,2.010\\,$ | $\\,2.405\\,$ | $\\,2.680\\,$ |\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32.5 & \\pm 2.682\\cdot \\frac{4.2}{\\sqrt{49}}\\,\\text{mpg} \\\\ 32.5 & \\pm 1.609\\,\\text{mpg}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186634560 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=49\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where  \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=32.5\\,\\text{mpg},\\,n=49,\\,s=4.2\\,\\text{mpg}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{48,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{48,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=49-1=48$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/23/2348635fd9697e8610533dbbfc019ba5da5b817381ce1c71cbfe6d74476deea7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{48,0.005}=2.682:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --