lpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=48,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.131)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.025}=2.131.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 48 & \\pm 4.262\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186634685 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=48,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.131)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.025}=2.131.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 48 & \\pm 4.262\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 175921