 by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.13=1.13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $8$ years, Mia will have $3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{8}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money Mia will have after $8$ years, we get\n\n$$\n3\\,500\\cdot (1.13)^{8}≈9\\,305\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar.\n\nHence, after $8$ years, Mia will have approximately $\\$9\\,305.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186636532 1071 "A particular culture initially has $256$ bacteria. If the population grows by $75%$ each hour, how many bacteria will there be in four hours?\n\nIn $4$ hours, there will be\n{{field-1}}\nbacteria in the culture." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186636532 1071 "A particular culture initially has $256$ bacteria. If the population grows by $75\\%$ each hour, how many bacteria will there be in four hours?\n\nIn $4$ hours, there will be\n{{field-1}}\nbacteria in the culture." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186636532 1074 "The culture starts with $256$ bacteria, and this amount increases by $75%$ each hour.\n\n$75%$ as a decimal is $0.75.$ Therefore, an increase of $75%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.75=1.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ hour, the culture has $256\\cdot (1.75)$ bacteria.\n\n- After $2$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{2}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $3$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{3}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $4$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}$ bacteria.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the population after $4$ hours, we get\n\n$$\n256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}=2\\,401.\n$$\n\nHence, after $4$ hours, there will be $2\\,401$ bacteria in the culture." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186636532 1074 "The culture starts with $256$ bacteria, and this amount increases by $75\\%$ each hour.\n\n$75\\%$ as a decimal is $0.75.$ Therefore, an increase of $75\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.75=1.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ hour, the culture has $256\\cdot (1.75)$ bacteria.\n\n- After $2$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{2}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $3$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{3}$ bacteria.\n\n- After $4$ hours, the culture will have $256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}$ bacteria.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the population after $4$ hours, we get\n\n$$\n256\\cdot (1.75)^{4}=2\\,401.\n$$\n\nHence, after $4$ hours, there will be $2\\,401$ bacteria in the culture." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186636541 1071 "Peter invests $\\$2\\,000$ in a savings account. The amount in the account increases by $11%$ each year. How much money will Peter have from this investment after $6$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186636541 1071 "Peter invests $\\$2\\,000$ in a savings account. The amount in the account increases by $11\\%$ each year. How much money will Peter have from this investment after $6$ years? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186636541 1074 "The savings account starts at $\\$2\\,000$, and this amount increases by $11%$ each year.\n\n$11%$ as a decimal is $0.11.$ Therefore, an increase of $11%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.11=1.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $6$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money Peter will have after $6$ years, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}≈3\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer value. \n\nHence, after $6$ years, Peter will have approximately $\\$3\\,741.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186636541 1074 "The savings account starts at $\\$2\\,000$, and this amount increases by $11\\%$ each year.\n\n$11\\%$ as a decimal is $0.11.$ Therefore, an increase of $11\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.11=1.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ year, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n$\\,⋮$\n\n- After $6$ years, Peter will have $2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the amount of money Peter will have after $6$ years, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (1.11)^{6}≈3\\,741\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer value. \n\nHence, after $6$ years, Peter will have approximately $\\$3\\,741.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637235 1071 "There are three groups of music lovers in a particular town: those who prefer jazz, those who prefer classical music, and those who prefer rock music. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer jazz, $10%$ change to classical music and $15%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer classical music, $5%$ change to jazz and $7%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer rock music, $15%$ change to jazz and $12%$ change to classical music.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & a \\\\ 0.1 & b & 0.12 \\\\ c & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637235 1071 "There are three groups of music lovers in a particular town: those who prefer jazz, those who prefer classical music, and those who prefer rock music. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer jazz, $10\\%$ change to classical music and $15\\%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer classical music, $5\\%$ change to jazz and $7\\%$ change to rock music,\n\n- from the people that prefer rock music, $15\\%$ change to jazz and $12\\%$ change to classical music.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & a \\\\ 0.1 & b & 0.12 \\\\ c & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637235 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the people that prefer jazz, $10%$ change to classical music and $15%$ change to rock music. This implies that $75%$ of them continue to prefer jazz.\n\n- From people that prefer classical music, $5%$ change to jazz and $7%$ change to rock music. This implies that $88%$ of them continue to prefer classical music.\n\n- From people that prefer rock music, $15%$ change to jazz and $12%$ change to classical music. This implies that $73%$ of them continue to prefer rock music.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.07$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.73$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.88 & 0.12 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.15+0.88+0.15=1.18.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637235 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the people that prefer jazz, $10\\%$ change to classical music and $15\\%$ change to rock music. This implies that $75\\%$ of them continue to prefer jazz.\n\n- From people that prefer classical music, $5\\%$ change to jazz and $7\\%$ change to rock music. This implies that $88\\%$ of them continue to prefer classical music.\n\n- From people that prefer rock music, $15\\%$ change to jazz and $12\\%$ change to classical music. This implies that $73\\%$ of them continue to prefer rock music.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Jazz</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Classics</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Rock</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.07$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.73$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 & 0.05 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.88 & 0.12 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.07 & 0.73\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.15+0.88+0.15=1.18.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637256 1071 "One month, the earnings of a shoe store were $\\$5\\,000.$ For all subsequent months, the store's earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny=5\\,000\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the store's earnings increase by $4%$ each month, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637256 1071 "One month, the earnings of a shoe store were $\\$5\\,000.$ For all subsequent months, the store's earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula:\n\n$$\ny=5\\,000\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\n$$\n\nIf the store's earnings increase by $4\\%$ each month, then what is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637256 1074 "The earnings $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $4%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=\\$5\\,000$ gives the store's initial earnings.\n\n- $r=0.04$ is the growth rate ($4%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.04)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.04)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.04.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637256 1074 "The earnings $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $4\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=\\$5\\,000$ gives the store's initial earnings.\n\n- $r=0.04$ is the growth rate ($4\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.04)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.04)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1.04.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637265 1071 "There are three groups of popcorn lovers among the customers of a certain cinema: those who prefer popcorn with salt, those who prefer popcorn with cheese, and those who prefer popcorn with caramel. A study has shown that each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with salt, $20%$ change to cheese flavor, and $10%$ change to caramel,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with cheese, $10%$ change to caramel flavor and $40%$ change to salt,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with caramel, $35%$ change to salt and $15%$ change to cheese.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.4 & a \\\\ 0.2 & b & 0.15 \\\\ c & 0.1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637265 1071 "There are three groups of popcorn lovers among the customers of a certain cinema: those who prefer popcorn with salt, those who prefer popcorn with cheese, and those who prefer popcorn with caramel. A study has shown that each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with salt, $20\\%$ change to cheese flavor, and $10\\%$ change to caramel,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with cheese, $10\\%$ change to caramel flavor and $40\\%$ change to salt,\n\n- from the customers that prefer popcorn with caramel, $35\\%$ change to salt and $15\\%$ change to cheese.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.4 & a \\\\ 0.2 & b & 0.15 \\\\ c & 0.1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637265 1074 "We are given the following situation for each month:\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with salt, $20%$ change to cheese, and $10%$ change to caramel. This implies that $70%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with salt.\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with cheese, $10%$ change to caramel and $40%$ change to salt. This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with cheese.\n\n- From the customers that prefer popcorn with caramel, $35%$ change to salt and $15%$ change to cheese. This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer popcorn with caramel.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Salt</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Cheese</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Caramel</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Salt</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Cheese</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Caramel</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 & 0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.5 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.1 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.35+0.5+0.1=0.95.\n$$" 13194139539448 1759218657