 [17592186637271 1071 "A certain company produces $2$ types of oil: vegetable oil and olive oil. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of oil each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $60%$ of vegetable oil customers switch to olive oil each month, and $35%$ of olive oil customers switch to vegetable oil.\n\nCurrently, $700$ customers use vegetable oil while $300$ customers use olive oil. How many customers will use olive oil in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637271 1071 "A certain company produces $2$ types of oil: vegetable oil and olive oil. The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of oil each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Vegetable Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Olive Oil</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $60\\%$ of vegetable oil customers switch to olive oil each month, and $35\\%$ of olive oil customers switch to vegetable oil.\n\nCurrently, $700$ customers use vegetable oil while $300$ customers use olive oil. How many customers will use olive oil in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637271 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of oil is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.385 \\\\ 0.615\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $61.5%$ of customers will use olive oil. Computing $61.5%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.615=615.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $615$ customers will use olive oil in one month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637271 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of oil is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}700 \\\\ 300\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.7 \\\\ 0.3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.385 \\\\ 0.615\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $61.5\\%$ of customers will use olive oil. Computing $61.5\\%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.615=615.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $615$ customers will use olive oil in one month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637276 1071 "At the time of its release, a new game already had 40 downloads. At the end of each day after release, the number of downloads increased by $150%$ of the number of downloads at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many downloads are there estimated to be after $2$ days?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637276 1071 "At the time of its release, a new game already had 40 downloads. At the end of each day after release, the number of downloads increased by $150\\%$ of the number of downloads at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many downloads are there estimated to be after $2$ days?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637276 1074 "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $150%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.5$ is the growth rate ($150%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after the release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=40\\cdot (1+1.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of downloads after $2$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=2:$\n\n$$\nD(2)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{2}=40\\cdot 6.25=250\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of downloads after $2$ days is $250.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637276 1074 "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $150\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.5$ is the growth rate ($150\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after the release\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=40\\cdot (1+1.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of downloads after $2$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=2:$\n\n$$\nD(2)=40\\cdot (2.5)^{2}=40\\cdot 6.25=250\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of downloads after $2$ days is $250.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186637282 1071 "Initially, an electronic store earned $\\$7\\,500$ per month. After an effective marketing campaign, the monthly earnings increased by $12%$ each month. \n\nThe store's monthly earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula\n\n$$\ny(t)=7\\,500\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186637282 1071 "Initially, an electronic store earned $\\$7\\,500$ per month. After an effective marketing campaign, the monthly earnings increased by $12\\%$ each month. \n\nThe store's monthly earnings $y(t),$ in dollars, after $t$ months is given by the formula\n\n$$\ny(t)=7\\,500\\cdot (\\text{___})^{t}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing value in the expression for $y?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 