he number of files to be analyzed $f(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=a(1-r)^{t}\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of files to be analyzed.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the decay rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in seconds.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $f$ is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\,000\\cdot (1-0.35)^{t}=2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how many seconds it takes to decrease to $5$ files, we need to solve the equation $f(t)=5.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}=5.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645042 1074 "The number of files to be analyzed $f(t)$ decreases exponentially with a decay rate of $35\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=a(1-r)^{t}\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of files to be analyzed.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the decay rate ($35\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in seconds.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $f$ is:\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\\,000\\cdot (1-0.35)^{t}=2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}\n$$\n\nSince we want to know how many seconds it takes to decrease to $5$ files, we need to solve the equation $f(t)=5.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\cdot (0.65)^{t}=5.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645057 1071 "The number of elephants in a herd reduces by $10%$ every year. Currently, there are $729$ elephants in the herd. How many elephants were in the herd $3$ years ago?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645057 1071 "The number of elephants in a herd reduces by $10\\%$ every year. Currently, there are $729$ elephants in the herd. How many elephants were in the herd $3$ years ago?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645057 1074 "The number of elephants $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of elephants (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.10$ is the decay rate ($10%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.10)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.90)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that there were $729$ elephants after $3$ years. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.90)^{3}=729.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{729}{(0.90)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{0.729} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645057 1074 "The number of elephants $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $10\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of elephants (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.10$ is the decay rate ($10\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.10)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.90)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that there were $729$ elephants after $3$ years. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.90)^{3}=729.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{729}{(0.90)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{0.729} \\\\  & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645092 1071 "Due to disease, the number of trees in a national park decreases by $40%$ every decade. Currently, there are $9\\,720$ trees. How many trees were in the park $5$ decades ago?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645092 1071 "Due to disease, the number of trees in a national park decreases by $40\\%$ every decade. Currently, there are $9\\,720$ trees. How many trees were in the park $5$ decades ago?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645092 1074 "The number of trees $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $40%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of trees (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.4$ is the decay rate ($40%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.4)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the number of trees was $9\\,720$ after $5$ decades. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.6)^{5}=9\\,720.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{9\\,720}{(0.60)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\,720}{0.07776} \\\\  & =125\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645092 1074 "The number of trees $y(t)$ decays exponentially with a decay rate of $40\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a$ is the initial number of trees (which is what we're trying to find).\n\n- $r=0.4$ is the decay rate ($40\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=a\\cdot (1-0.4)^{t}=a\\cdot (0.6)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the number of trees was $9\\,720$ after $5$ decades. Therefore,\n\n$$\na\\cdot (0.6)^{5}=9\\,720.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{9\\,720}{(0.60)^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\,720}{0.07776} \\\\  & =125\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645718 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $16$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645718 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $16$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25\\%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186645718 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=16)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{0})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{16}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{2})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.75)^{16}+16(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+120(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-0.197\\,111 \\\\  & =0.803\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186645718 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers at least $3$ questions correctly. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=16)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{0})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{16}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{2})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.75)^{16}+16(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{15}+120(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{14}] \\\\  & =1-0.197\\,111 \\\\  & =0.803\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646277 1071 "When a science blog was first posted, it had $30$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after posting, the number of views increased by $110%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous day.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ days after the blog was posted, and $t\\le 6?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646277 1071 "When a science blog was first posted, it had $30$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after posting, the number of views increased by $110\\%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous day.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ days after the blog was posted, and $t\\le 6?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646277 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $110%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=30$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.1$ is the growth rate ($110%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after posting\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=30\\cdot (1+1.1)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=30\\cdot (2.1)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646277 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $110\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=30$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.1$ is the growth rate ($110\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after posting\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=30\\cdot (1+1.1)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=30\\cdot (2.1)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646294 1071 "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646294 1071 "Find ${M}^{T}$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646297 1071 "In a tropical forest, there are $150$ Malachite Kingfishers. Every year, the number of Kingfishers increases by $30%.$ What function represents the number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ after $t$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646297 1071 "In a tropical forest, there are $150$ Malachite Kingfishers. Every year, the number of Kingfishers increases by $30\\%.$ What function represents the number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ after $t$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646297 1074 "The number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=150$ is the initial number of Kingfishers.\n\n- $b=0.3$ is the growth rate ($30%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =150\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =150\\cdot {1.3}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646297 1074 "The number of Kingfishers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=150$ is the initial number of Kingfishers.\n\n- $b=0.3$ is the growth rate ($30\\%$ expressed as a percent).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =150\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =150\\cdot {1.3}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646343 1071 "When a music video was first uploaded, it had $80$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $200%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the video was uploaded, and $t\\le 4?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646343 1071 "When a music video was first uploaded, it had $80$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after upload, the number of views increased by $200\\%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the video was uploaded, and $t\\le 4?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186646343 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $200%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=80$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.0$ is the growth rate ($200%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=80\\cdot (1+2.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=80\\cdot (3)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186646343 1074 "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $200\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=80$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=2.0$ is the growth rate ($200\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after upload\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=80\\cdot (1+2.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=80\\cdot (3)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186647093 1071 "Find ${R}^{T}$ given that \n    $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186647093 1071 "Find ${R}^{T}$ given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186648615 1071 "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $38%$ of all males smoke. Overall, $27.3%$ of the population smoke, and $49%$ of the population are male. If a smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186648615 1071 "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $38\\%$ of all males smoke. Overall, $27.3\\%$ of the population smoke, and $49\\%$ of the population are male. If a smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186648615 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that a randomly selected person smokes, and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|S)=\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(S|M)=0.38,$ since $38%$ of all males smoke.\n\n- $P(M)=0.49,$ since $49%$ of the population are male.\n\n- $P(S)=0.273,$ since $27%$ of the population smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|S) & =\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.38)(0.49)}{0.273} \\\\  & ≈68%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186648615 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that a randomly selected person smokes, and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|S)=\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(S|M)=0.38,$ since $38\\%$ of all males smoke.\n\n- $P(M)=0.49,$ since $49\\%$ of the population are male.\n\n- $P(S)=0.273,$ since $27\\%$ of the population smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|S) & =\\frac{P(S|M)P(M)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.38)(0.49)}{0.273} \\\\  & ≈68\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186648679 1071 "The lifetime of bulbs produced by a pa