 per year, and the time is $12$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n9\\%=9÷100=0.09\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =3,500\\times 0.09\\times 12 \\\\  & =3,780.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$3,780.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n3,500+3,780=7,280\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$7,280.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186652309 1074 "The principal is $\\$3,100,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n4,495-3,100=1,395\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,395.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,395 & =3,100\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,395 & =18,600r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1395}{18,600} \\\\ r & =0.075\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.075$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.075=0.075\\times 100%=7.5%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $7.5%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186652309 1074 "The principal is $\\$3,100,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n4,495-3,100=1,395\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,395.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,395 & =3,100\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,395 & =18,600r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1395}{18,600} \\\\ r & =0.075\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.075$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.075=0.075\\times 100\\%=7.5\\%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $7.5\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186652318 1074 "The principal is $\\$5,000,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n6,050-5,000=1,050\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,050.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,050 & =5,000\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,050 & =30,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,050}{30,000} \\\\ r & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.035$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.035=0.035\\times 100%=3.5%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $3.5%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186652318 1074 "The principal is $\\$5,000,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n6,050-5,000=1,050\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,050.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,050 & =5,000\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,050 & =30,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,050}{30,000} \\\\ r & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.035$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.035=0.035\\times 100\\%=3.5\\%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $3.5\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186652912 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $80$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.797)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186652912 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $80$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.797)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186652912 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=80,\\,n=25,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.797)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.005}=2.797.\n$$\n\n![](images/4f/4f9a0b7c9ec55a5c061f709b2d6fa02c3f760ccd66faab24c1201c4cf48bb18c.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 80\\pm 2.797\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & 80\\pm 3.356\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186652912 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distri