 eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.82,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.977,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.378.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.82}^{2}}{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}+{0.378}^{2}}≈66.2\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}}{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}+{0.378}^{2}}≈98.8\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.2\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.8\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/72/724b328795c44214650622f8fb6cdf28b8b56a9b6033b75434da6e83f373f10c.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670007 1074 "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $4$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,480-2,000=480\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$480.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 480 & =2,000\\times r\\times 4 \\\\ 480 & =8,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{480}{8,000} \\\\ r & =0.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.06$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.06=0.06\\times 100%=6%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $6%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670007 1074 "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $4$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,480-2,000=480\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$480.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 480 & =2,000\\times r\\times 4 \\\\ 480 & =8,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{480}{8,000} \\\\ r & =0.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.06$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.06=0.06\\times 100\\%=6\\%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $6\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670013 1071 "Kathryn answered $88%$ of the questions correctly on her last trigonometry test. What fraction of questions did she answer correctly?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670013 1071 "Kathryn answered $88\\%$ of the questions correctly on her last trigonometry test. What fraction of questions did she answer correctly?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
